/**
 * RenewalReminderService
 *
 * Sends a plain-text renewal reminder to the Owner of each Opportunity whose
 * renewal is due and returns the number of reminders that were sent
 * successfully.
 *
 * Design decisions
 *  - One Messaging.sendEmail() call per Opportunity (a one-element list), issued
 *    inside the loop and wrapped in try/catch. A single batched
 *    Messaging.sendEmail(messages) call is all-or-nothing by default, so one bad
 *    recipient would cancel every reminder in the batch.
 *  - The recipient is addressed with setTargetObjectId(<Owner Id>) and NOT with
 *    setToAddresses(<Owner.Email>):
 *      * Salesforce resolves the address at send time, so the service never
 *        reads, exposes or caches a user's e-mail address,
 *      * mail to an internal User is not charged against the org's daily
 *        allocation for e-mails to external recipients.
 *  - setSaveAsActivity(false): no Task/Event is created for the recipient.
 *  - Bulk safe: ids are de-duplicated and processed in chunks of 200 (well
 *    inside the 1,000 bind-variable limit), so one bad chunk or one bad
 *    recipient never stops the rest of the run.
 */
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /** Maximum number of Opportunities queried/sent per chunk. */
    private static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 200;

    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Renewal due: ';

    /** Platform limit for an e-mail subject. */
    private static final Integer MAX_SUBJECT_LENGTH = 255;

    /** Platform limit for an e-mail body. */
    private static final Integer MAX_BODY_LENGTH = 32000;

    /**
     * Sends a renewal reminder to the owner of every supplied Opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the Opportunities whose renewal is due.
     * @return the number of e-mails that were sent successfully.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // De-duplicate so the same opportunity can never be reminded twice in
        // one run (the caller may hand us a list that already contains dups).
        Set<Id> uniqueIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                uniqueIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (uniqueIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Chunk the ids so the query always stays inside the 1,000 bind-variable
        // limit, no matter how many ids the caller passes in.
        List<List<Id>> idChunks = new List<List<Id>>();
        List<Id> currentChunk = new List<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : uniqueIds) {
            currentChunk.add(opportunityId);
            if (currentChunk.size() >= CHUNK_SIZE) {
                idChunks.add(currentChunk);
                currentChunk = new List<Id>();
            }
        }
        if (!currentChunk.isEmpty()) {
            idChunks.add(currentChunk);
        }

        Integer sentCount = 0;
        for (List<Id> chunkIds : idChunks) {
            sentCount += sendChunk(chunkIds);
        }

        return sentCount;
    }

    /**
     * Queries one chunk of Opportunities and sends one reminder per owner.
     */
    private static Integer sendChunk(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        List<Opportunity> opportunities = queryOpportunities(opportunityIds);

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        for (Opportunity opportunity : opportunities) {
            if (opportunity.OwnerId == null) {
                log(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                    'RenewalReminderService: Opportunity ' + opportunity.Id
                    + ' has no owner; reminder skipped.');
                continue;
            }

            try {
                Messaging.SingleEmailMessage message = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();

                // Address the internal owner by Id: the address is resolved by
                // Salesforce and internal mail does not consume the daily
                // external-mail allocation.
                message.setTargetObjectId(opportunity.OwnerId);

                message.setSubject(buildSubject(opportunity));
                message.setPlainTextBody(buildBody(opportunity));

                // No Task/Event is created for the recipient.
                message.setSaveAsActivity(false);

                // One call per opportunity on purpose: a failure here must not
                // stop the remaining reminders from going out. The Apex API
                // only exposes the List overload of sendEmail, so we pass a
                // single-element list.
                Messaging.sendEmail(new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>{ message });
                sentCount++;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Log and keep going - a single bad reminder must never abort
                // the rest of the batch.
                log(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'RenewalReminderService: Failed to send renewal reminder for Opportunity '
                    + opportunity.Id + ' - ' + e.getTypeName() + ': ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }

    /**
     * Reads the Opportunities of one chunk. Runs in USER_MODE so CRUD/FLS is
     * enforced instead of being silently bypassed. A query failure only fails
     * its own chunk; the remaining chunks are still processed.
     */
    private static List<Opportunity> queryOpportunities(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        try {
            return [
                SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
                FROM Opportunity
                WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
                WITH USER_MODE
            ];
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'RenewalReminderService: Could not query ' + opportunityIds.size()
                + ' opportunit(y/ies) - ' + e.getTypeName() + ': ' + e.getMessage());
            return new List<Opportunity>();
        }
    }

    /**
     * Builds 'Renewal due: <opportunity name>', truncated to the platform limit.
     */
    private static String buildSubject(Opportunity opportunity) {
        String subject = SUBJECT_PREFIX + (opportunity.Name == null ? '' : opportunity.Name);
        if (subject.length() > MAX_SUBJECT_LENGTH) {
            subject = subject.substring(0, MAX_SUBJECT_LENGTH);
        }
        return subject;
    }

    /**
     * Builds the plain-text body, including the close date.
     */
    private static String buildBody(Opportunity opportunity) {
        // Apex Date has no format() method (only Datetime does), so the ISO
        // yyyy-MM-dd representation of the Date is used directly.
        String closeDate = opportunity.CloseDate == null
            ? ''
            : String.valueOf(opportunity.CloseDate);

        String body =
            'Hello,' + '\n\n'
            + 'This is a reminder that the following opportunity is due for renewal.' + '\n\n'
            + 'Opportunity: ' + (opportunity.Name == null ? '' : opportunity.Name) + '\n'
            + 'Opportunity Id: ' + opportunity.Id + '\n'
            + 'Close date: ' + (closeDate == '' ? 'not set' : closeDate) + '\n\n'
            + 'Please contact the customer and confirm the renewal.' + '\n\n'
            + 'Thanks,' + '\n'
            + 'Renewal Reminders';

        if (body.length() > MAX_BODY_LENGTH) {
            body = body.substring(0, MAX_BODY_LENGTH);
        }
        return body;
    }

    /**
     * Minimal logging hook. Replace with your platform logging framework
     * (Logger / custom object / platform event) for production diagnostics.
     */
    private static void log(LoggingLevel level, String message) {
        System.debug(level, message);
    }
}
```

**Fix applied to the previous version**

- `Date.format(String)` does not exist in Apex — only `Datetime` has a `format()` method. The close date is now rendered with `String.valueOf(opportunity.CloseDate)`, which produces the ISO `yyyy-MM-dd` form. (If a locale-specific format is ever needed, convert to a `Datetime` with `Datetime.newInstance(d, TimeZone.getTimeZone('UTC'))` and call `format()` on that instead.)

**Review of your approach (unchanged conclusions)**

Good, keep it:

- `setTargetObjectId(<owner Id>)` + `setSaveAsActivity(false)` is exactly right: Salesforce resolves the internal address at send time (you never read `User.Email` into a variable), internal mail doesn't consume the daily external-mail allocation, and no Task/Event noise is created.
- One `sendEmail` call per opportunity inside the loop is correct — a single batched `Messaging.sendEmail(messages)` is all-or-nothing by default, so one undeliverable recipient would cancel the whole batch. `sendEmail(messages, false)` isn't a good substitute: it throws at the end and returns no per-message result, so you can't tell which reminders actually went out. (Apex only exposes the `List<SingleEmailMessage>` overload, hence the one-element list per opportunity.)
- ≤200 opportunities per call is comfortably inside the 1,000 bind-variable limit and governor limits.

Added/changed, and why:

1. **De-duplication + chunking** — a duplicated id in the scheduler's list would otherwise produce two identical reminders; chunking keeps bind variables and transaction size safe even if someone later passes more than 200 ids.
2. **Subject truncated to 255 chars** — an Opportunity Name can be 255 characters, so `'Renewal due: ' + Name` would exceed the platform subject limit and fail for the longest names. Body truncated at 32,000 chars for the same reason.
3. **`try/catch` per opportunity with logging** — silently swallowing exceptions leaves you with "180 of 200 sent" and no idea which 20 failed; each failure is logged with the Opportunity Id and exception type/message.
4. **Query failures isolated per chunk** and logged, so one bad id list can't abort the whole job.
5. **`WITH USER_MODE`** enforces CRUD/FLS instead of bypassing them; the scheduled job's running user must therefore have read access to `Opportunity` and a valid, monitored e-mail address — use a dedicated service account, not a human's account, and consider `setSenderDisplayName`.

Watch out for:

- **E-mails are not rolled back with the transaction.** If the transaction later fails and is retried, reminders already sent are not undone → duplicates. Make it idempotent by stamping the opportunity (e.g. `Last_Renewal_Reminder__c`) and skipping records already reminded this week.
- **Don't run this synchronously in a trigger** — keep it in the scheduled/async context, or move it to a Queueable/Batch for thousands of reminders (one long transaction with many `sendEmail` calls is a governor-limit and lock-contention risk).
- **Test-context limit:** Apex tests allow only ~10 email invocations per transaction, so keep test data small.
- **`System.debug` is not production logging** — replace the `log()` helper with a real logging framework (Logger / custom object / platform event).
- **"Renewal due" is assumed to mean `Opportunity.CloseDate`.** If renewal actually lives on a custom field (e.g. `Renewal_Date__c`), use it in both the body and the scheduler's filter, otherwise the reminder text contradicts the date the job filters on.

## Fix

The page reference types were wrong for two of the actions. The related list must use `standard__recordRelationshipPage` and the edit page must use `standard__recordPage` (the `standard__objectPage` type with a `recordId` attribute is not valid for those actions). The New Contact case correctly uses `standard__objectPage` with `actionName: 'new'`.

