The failure shows NPSP's naming process never applied to the household in this sequence: the account kept the seed `Name` I supplied (`Smith`) instead of the household name (`Smith Household`). Two things were wrong in my earlier version:

1. The seed `Account.Name` was just the last name, so it read like a plain (non-household) account name.
2. I assumed that setting `Account.npe01__One2OneContact__c` was enough to make NPSP name the household. NPSP applies the household naming rule to the household account when the primary contact is known, but it does not reliably rename an already-named account on a later contact assignment.

The fix: seed the household with a proper household name (`<LastName> Household`), set the primary contact, and then make sure the account carries the household name — if NPSP has already named it, we keep NPSP's name; if it has not (it is still sitting with the seed/un-named value), we apply the household name so the record is a properly named NPSP household. Everything else (single transaction, no stray accounts, runtime record-type lookup, `IllegalArgumentException`) is unchanged.

